; Deep compiled->compiled recursion through a function with LARGE frames (hundreds of locals in the method
; that makes the recursive call). The recursion budget must account for frame size: a fixed per-call charge
; let the Java stack overflow below the guard (the function was then switched to vanilla with partial side
; effects). The tellraws target nobody; they only make the frame large at a low vanilla command cost, so the
; whole run stays below vanilla's max_command_sequence_length (65536), which compiled code doesn't model.
@setup
objective create t dummy

@function run
scoreboard players set #n t 0
scoreboard players set #a0 t 0
scoreboard players set #a1 t 1
scoreboard players set #a2 t 2
scoreboard players set #a3 t 3
scoreboard players set #a4 t 4
scoreboard players set #a5 t 5
scoreboard players set #a6 t 6
scoreboard players set #a7 t 7
scoreboard players set #a8 t 8
scoreboard players set #a9 t 9
scoreboard players set #a10 t 10
scoreboard players set #a11 t 11
function mcfc-test:rec

@function rec
scoreboard players add #n t 1
tellraw @a[tag=mcfc_nobody] [{"score":{"name":"#a0","objective":"t"}},{"score":{"name":"#a1","objective":"t"}},{"score":{"name":"#a2","objective":"t"}},{"score":{"name":"#a3","objective":"t"}},{"score":{"name":"#a4","objective":"t"}},{"score":{"name":"#a5","objective":"t"}}]
